  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "Define a sequence of real numbers \\(a_1,\\) \\(a_2,\\) \\(a_3,\\) \\(\\ldots\\) by \\(a_1 = 1\\) and \\(a_{n+1}^3 = 99 a_n^3\\) for all \\(n \\ge 1.\\) Then \\(a_{100}\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(33^{33}\\)",
    "B": "\\(33^{99}\\)",
    "C": "\\(99^{33}\\)",
    "D": "\\(99^{99}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The recurrence gives a simple multiplication rule for the cubes. Define \\(b_n=a_n^3\\) for \\(n\\ge1\\). Then\n\\[\nb_1=1,\\qquad b_{n+1}=99b_n.\n\\]\nEach step introduces one factor of \\(99\\). Reaching term \\(n\\) from term \\(1\\) takes \\(n-1\\) steps, so \\(b_n=99^{n-1}\\). In particular,\n\\[\na_{100}^3=b_{100}=99^{99}=(99^{33})^3.\n\\]\nEvery real number has a unique real cube root, so \\(a_{100}=99^{33}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
